== 1. the swing reaction cannot be shaped away ==
  swing-link mass (thigh+calf+foot) 4.6789 kg
  effective lateral mass at the foot 2.1522 kg (0.4600 of the link mass), cross terms (+0.1011, -, +0.3557) kg
  optimised horizontal window 0.1046 s -> quantised 0.11 s for a 60 mm step
  per-leg minimum peak foot acceleration 19.8347 m/s^2, summed-path minimum 39.6694 m/s^2
  two in-phase bang-bang legs give exactly 39.6694 m/s^2 of summed acceleration, which equals the summed-path minimum: the bound is attained, so no staggering, phase offset or reshaping can lower the peak
  peak lateral reaction on the body 85.4 N, 18.1% of the 472 N body weight
  quintic for comparison needs 28.6289 m/s^2 per leg, so the minimum-time profile is also the gentlest available at this window
  therefore the only ways to cut the reaction are a shorter step or a longer window, both of which are slower: this is a speed/disturbance trade, not something a policy can optimise away

== 2. the reaction is a feedforward term, not a learning problem ==
  the frozen body force law is m*(g + a_ref + 26*(p_ref-p) + 9*(v_ref-vCOM));
  it carries no swing-reaction term, so the reaction enters as an unmodelled
  disturbance that only the body PD rejects.
  but the swing plan is known at latch time, so -sum(m_i * a_i,ref) is exactly
  computable: 85.4 N of feedforward, leaving only tracking error
    if the swing legs track their reference to 5%, the residual disturbance is 4.3 N, 0.9% of body weight
    if the swing legs track their reference to 10%, the residual disturbance is 8.5 N, 1.8% of body weight
    if the swing legs track their reference to 20%, the residual disturbance is 17.1 N, 3.6% of body weight
  this is a seventh reference-level correction and it is free; it should be in
  the fixed baseline, not delegated to a policy

== 3. what is actually state-dependent: the contact-admission wait ==
  the frozen gates that can stall a pair, from contract35 and clarifications:
    lift admission: both swing wheels whole-shape min Z > 12 mm AND no active
      terrain contact in all five previous native substeps
    transfer->lift: at least 0.06 s AND current plus each previous five substeps
      satisfy the intended stance-pair load gate, hold at most 0.40 s more
    touchdown: actual positive contact on both returning wheels, then the dwell,
      with at most 0.60 s of extra allowance
     wait/pair      speed  vs zero wait   floor   goal
          0 ct    0.07317         +0.0%      ok     ok
          2 ct    0.06977         -4.7%      ok     ok
          5 ct    0.06522        -10.9%      ok     ok
         10 ct    0.05882        -19.6%      ok     ok
         15 ct    0.05357        -26.8%      ok     ok
         20 ct    0.04918        -32.8%      ok     ok
         30 ct    0.04225        -42.3%      ok     ok
         40 ct    0.03704        -49.4%      ok   FAIL
  a policy cannot remove a gate, but it can shape the approach so the gate is
  satisfied sooner: slower, flatter touchdown for cleaner load onset, and a
  liftoff that clears 12 mm with less overshoot. The table is the price list.

== 4. the RL contribution gate, computed ==
  ideal fixed baseline, zero wait: fixed 0.07317 m/s -> RL must exceed 0.08780 m/s to clear the 20% gate
    with zero wait there is no admission headroom left, so the gate cannot be cleared by closing waits at all
  plausible fixed baseline, 10 controls: fixed 0.05882 m/s -> RL must exceed 0.07059 m/s to clear the 20% gate
    closing the wait entirely would give 0.07317 m/s, +24.4%: gate reachable by admission alone
  pessimistic fixed baseline, 20 controls: fixed 0.04918 m/s -> RL must exceed 0.05902 m/s to clear the 20% gate
    closing the wait entirely would give 0.07317 m/s, +48.8%: gate reachable by admission alone

== 5. scope for a bounded RL contract ==
  the policy MAY propose, inside the frozen envelope:
    - a residual on the landing target inside the unchanged 60 mm ball
    - a residual on the touchdown approach speed and the lower-phase profile
    - a residual on the lift profile subject to the unchanged 12 mm clearance
    - the decision to extend a dwell instead of aborting a marginal pair
  the policy MAY NOT:
    - change any contact, load, clearance, pose, torque or no-flight gate
    - change the integration, the actuator limits or the landing cap
    - choose the step length or the commanded velocity (those are the operator's)
    - replace the swing-reaction feedforward of section 2, which belongs to the
      fixed baseline
  the reward must be speed per qualified pair exchange with the full safety and
  retention gates as hard terminations, and the comparison must be against the
  SAME corrected fixed controller, not against the frozen 0.0236 m/s baseline
  a bounded budget in the established shape, to be frozen by the next contract:
    smoke: 64 episodes x 1800 controls = 115200 controls, 576000 normal native substeps
    development: 512 episodes x 1800 controls = 921600 controls, 4608000 normal native substeps
  this is a proposal only: source_go35.json carries training_authorized false and its single physical attempt is consumed, so no training may start without a new Astra-signed GO.
